## 🟠 Medium-Severity Findings

### M1. Missing security headers (OWASP A05: Security Misconfiguration)

Express by default sends `X-Powered-By: Express` (information disclosure) and sets none of the standard hardening headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, etc.).

**Recommendation** — use `helmet`:

```js
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.disable('x-powered-by');
app.use(helmet());
```

### M2. No rate limiting / DoS protection (OWASP A04: Insecure Design)

Both endpoints can be hit unboundedly. `/users` in particular can be scraped trivially.

**Recommendation**:

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
app.use(rateLimit({ windowMs: 60_000, max: 100 }));
```

### M3. No CORS policy declared (OWASP A05)

Without an explicit CORS configuration, browser behavior is the only thing preventing cross-origin reads. If a future change adds `app.use(cors())` carelessly, it will become a wide-open API.

**Recommendation** — be explicit and restrictive:

```js
const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://your-frontend.example.com'],
  methods: ['GET'],
  credentials: false,
}));
```

### M4. Unconditional `app.listen()` makes testing harder and binds to all interfaces

`app.listen(3000, ...)` runs on `require()` and binds to `0.0.0.0` by default. In containerized/dev environments this can unintentionally expose the service on a LAN.

**Recommendation**:

```js
if (require.main === module) {
  const host = process.env.HOST || '127.0.0.1';
  const port = Number(process.env.PORT) || 3000;
  app.listen(port, host, () => console.log(`Server running on ${host}:${port}`));
}
```

